  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q59-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "59",
    "prompt": "Show that if $\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac\\pi4$, then\n$$(1+\\tan\\alpha)(1+\\tan\\beta)=2.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(1+\\tan\\alpha)(1+\\tan\\beta)=2$.\n\nUse $\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)=1$ and rearrange the tangent sum expansion.",
      "worked": "Since $\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac\\pi4$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\tan\\frac\\pi4=1,\\\\\\frac{\\tan\\alpha+\\tan\\beta}{1-\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta}&=1,\\\\\\tan\\alpha+\\tan\\beta&=1-\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}(1+\\tan\\alpha)(1+\\tan\\beta)&=1+\\tan\\alpha+\\tan\\beta+\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta\\\\(1+\\tan\\alpha)(1+\\tan\\beta)&=1+\\bigl(1-\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta\\bigr)+\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta\\\\(1+\\tan\\alpha)(1+\\tan\\beta)&=2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
